  "name": "Question 5",
  "problem": "The quadratic coefficient of a particular quadratic equation equals $1.$ The sum of the solutions equals $7,$ and the product of the solutions equals $12.$ Find the equation.\n\nThe equation is\n{{field-1}}\n$=0.$",
  "worked_solution": "Suppose the quadratic equation\n\n$$\na{x}^{2}+bx+c=0\n$$\n\nhas the solutions $x={x}_{1}$ and $x={x}_{2},$ where $a\\ne 0.$ \n\nThen, Vieta's formulas state the following:\n\n- The sum of the solutions is given by\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=-\\frac{b}{a}.\n$$\n\n- The product of the solutions is given by\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=\\frac{c}{a}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's use the given information to determine the values of $a,b,$ and $c$ in this case:\n\n- We're told that the quadratic coefficient equals $1.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\na=1.\n$$\n\n- We're told that the sum of the roots equals $7.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7 & =-\\frac{b}{a} \\\\ 7 & =-\\frac{b}{1} \\\\ -7 & =b \\\\ b & =-7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- We're told that the product of the roots equals $12.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12 & =\\frac{c}{a} \\\\ 12 & =\\frac{c}{1} \\\\ c & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our equation has coefficients\n\n$$\na=1,\\,b=-7,\\,c=12\n$$\n\nand the equation is\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-7x+12=0.\n$$",
